{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"LEAF 1 COMPLETE: 6f18 (unacademic-diabolic-esok) closed after both changes merged (2046 -> 55e308eaaf, 2048 -> 597010b9f1), each LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1. Close passed the completion-verification gate AND the execution-phase plan-review close gate; it unblocked 1cf6. PARALLELISM CORRECTION (user challenge): the earlier 'concurrency ceiling of 3-4' was an unfounded inference from a single store-read-failure under unknown load with 4+ other sessions competing. The real, reproducible blocker is a git ref race - concurrent review-plan runs in ONE checkout each git-fetch and stomp refs/remotes/origin/tickets ('cannot lock ref: is at X but expected Y'); worktrees do not fix it since they share refs/. Mitigation: stagger dispatch 40s so only the brief fetch serialises. Now running 5-8 concurrent reviews cleanly. Investigation+remediation fanned out to read-only subagents that WRITE a full corrected description to tmp/remediation/<id>.md; orchestrator applies rebar edit serially. 14 of 23 plans corrected this way; every single one had material factual errors."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787368941209205001,"uuid":"3af02c2a-fae2-48b8-8825-bb84c1c957fb"}